codex-plugin: start a task turn with native skill input and a network-capable sandbox policy

Target: the codex@openai-codex Claude Code plugin (any 1.0.x; applied with
context matching, not line numbers). Independent of the queue passthrough
patch: it touches scripts/lib/codex.mjs only.

Why:
  The companion's task route builds turn/start input as one text item and
  fixes the sandbox at thread creation ("read-only", or "workspace-write" with
  --write). Neither can express a required skill, and neither can say "this
  reviewer may read GitHub". The Codex app-server supports both on turn/start:
  a {type:"skill", name, path} input item (documented as the way to invoke a
  skill explicitly), and sandboxPolicy, whose readOnly and workspaceWrite
  variants carry a separate networkAccess flag.

What this changes:
  When CREW_CODEX_TURN_SPEC names a JSON file ({"skills":[{name,path}],
  "network":bool}) written by crew-codex after it validated the skills:
  1. turn/start input carries "$name" mentions plus one skill item per skill.
  2. network:true sends sandboxPolicy readOnly+networkAccess, or
     workspaceWrite+networkAccess when the task was launched with --write.
  3. The thread the turn runs on is written to "<spec file>.thread", so the
     launcher can tie the requirements to the job that owns that thread.
  4. A spec may carry "resumeThread": the thread crew-codex validated the
     requirements for. If the companion's own resume selection names another
     thread, the run throws before any thread is resumed or published.
  With the variable unset, behavior is stock. A set but unreadable or
  malformed spec throws instead of running without the requirement.

Network is not write authority and write is not network: approvalPolicy and the
thread sandbox are untouched.

Reverse with: crew-codex patch --revert

